package Vixen.Shaders.PostFx

import Vixen.Shaders.Core
import Vixen.Shaders.Geometry

// Local exposure: one exposure per region of the frame rather than one for the whole of it.
//
// **The thing a single exposure cannot do.** A frame containing a sunlit window and an unlit interior
// has a dynamic range of ten or twelve stops between the two, and a tone curve has about six to spend.
// Whatever exposure the meter picks, one of them is white or black — which is exactly what a camera
// does and exactly what an eye does not, because an eye adapts locally. Unreal 5 calls this Local
// Exposure and it is the one effect in their list that has no Unity counterpart at all.
//
// **A base/detail split, which is the whole idea in one line.** Blur the log luminance to get the
// *base* — the slow, large-scale brightness of each region — and what is left over is the *detail*,
// the local contrast that carries texture and edges. Compress the base and leave the detail alone, and
// the window comes down eight stops while the grain of the wall beside it is untouched. Compress the
// image itself instead and you get the flat, grey, HDR-photograph look everybody recognises, because
// that compresses the detail too.
//
// **The blur has to be edge-aware, and that is the expensive part.** A plain Gaussian base bleeds the
// window's brightness across its frame, so the wall next to it is compressed as though it were bright
// — the dark halo around every high-contrast edge that people call "the HDR halo". A bilateral filter
// weights each tap by how close its luminance is to the centre's as well as by distance, so the
// window's texels do not contribute to the wall's base at all.
//
// ⚠ **Two modes, because the base has to exist before it can be applied**, and a fragment stage
// cannot write a texture it is also reading:
//
//   Blur   builds the base: a bilateral gather over log luminance, at reduced resolution.
//   Apply  reads the base at full resolution and re-composes.
//
// ⚠ **Scene-referred, before the tonemap.** It moves radiance around before the curve shapes it —
// which is the point, since the whole purpose is to bring the highlights into the range the curve can
// spend. After the curve there is nothing left to recover.

shader LocalExposure {
    /// 0 builds the base, 1 applies it.
    [Permutation] val Mode: int = 0

    /// How many taps the bilateral blur uses along each axis of its cross.
    ///
    /// A permutation so the loop unrolls. The gather is a cross rather than a box — 4N taps rather
    /// than N² — which is a real approximation and is the reason this runs at reduced resolution,
    /// where a cross of a given texel radius covers a much larger part of the image.
    [Permutation] val Taps: int = 6

    var source: Texture2D
    var sourceSampler: Sampler

    /// The blurred log luminance, read by `Apply`. Its own target in `Blur`.
    var baseLuminance: Texture2D
    var baseSampler: Sampler

    /// One texel of whatever this pass is reading.
    var texelSize: float2 = float2(0.001f, 0.001f)

    /// How far the bilateral gather reaches, in texels of the reduced image.
    var blurRadius: float = 6f

    /// How different two luminances have to be, in stops, before a tap stops counting.
    ///
    /// ⚠ This is what makes the filter bilateral rather than Gaussian, and it is the one number that
    /// decides whether the result has halos. Large, and the window bleeds across its frame; small, and
    /// the base follows every edge and there is no large-scale brightness left to compress.
    var edgeRange: float = 1.5f

    /// How much the highlights are brought down. 0 is off, 1 flattens them onto the base entirely.
    var highlightContrast: float = 0.6f

    /// And how much the shadows are brought up.
    ///
    /// Separate from the highlights because the two are not symmetric in what they cost: lifting a
    /// shadow raises whatever noise is in it, and pulling a highlight down does not.
    var shadowContrast: float = 0.4f

    /// Linear exposure multiplier, when it is authored rather than measured.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **The pivot is derived from this rather than handed over, and that is the fix for a whole
    /// class of being wrong.** The pivot is the scene luminance that comes out as middle grey, which
    /// is `0.18 / exposure` and nothing else — a host computing it separately is a second derivation
    /// of one number, and the first one drifted by 2.7 stops without a single test noticing, because
    /// what a wrong pivot produces is a picture rather than a failure.
    var exposure: float = 1f

    /// What `AutoExposure` left for this frame. One element, read only when `UseExposureBuffer`.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Read here for exactly `Tonemap.rvn`'s reason.** A metered frame's exposure is produced on
    /// the device and never crosses the bus, so a host cannot know it — and this pass runs *only* in
    /// a metered frame, because `post.localExposure` is gated on the meter. A pivot the host supplied
    /// would therefore be wrong in every frame this shader has ever run in.
    var exposureBuffer: Buffer<float>

    /// Whether the exposure comes from the buffer rather than from `exposure`.
    ///
    /// ⚠ A permutation, and — as always — it folds the *read* and not the *binding*: both variants
    /// declare `exposureBuffer`, so a frame that leaves it unmetered still owes the slot a buffer.
    [Permutation] val UseExposureBuffer: bool = false

    /// A ceiling on how far any pixel may move, in stops.
    ///
    /// The safety rail. Without it a pixel whose base is far from its own luminance — the inside of a
    /// thin bright object, where the bilateral filter has almost no like-valued neighbours — can be
    /// moved arbitrarily far and reads as a bright or dark speck.
    var maximumStops: float = 4f

    stream var uv: float2

    [VertexShader]
    [Semantic("SV_Position")]
    func Vertex([Semantic("SV_VertexID")] vertexIndex: int): float4 {
        uv = Fullscreen.Uv(vertexIndex)
        return Fullscreen.Position(vertexIndex)
    }

    [FragmentShader]
    [Semantic("SV_Target")]
    func Fragment(): float4 {
        if (Mode == 0) {
            return float4(Blur(), 0f, 0f, 1f)
        }

        return Apply()
    }

    /// The bilateral base at this texel: a weighted mean of the log luminance around it.
    func Blur(): float {
        val centre = LogLuminance(source.Sample(sourceSampler, uv).rgb)

        var total = centre
        var weight = 1f

        // A cross rather than a box. Four taps per step instead of the ring's eight or the box's
        // square, which at six steps is 24 taps rather than 168 — and at reduced resolution the
        // difference between a cross and a box is a base that is very slightly directional, against a
        // pass that costs seven times as much.
        for (i in 1 .. Taps) {
            val step = float(i) / float(Taps) * blurRadius

            total += Tap(float2(step, 0f), centre, weight)
            total += Tap(float2(-step, 0f), centre, weight)
            total += Tap(float2(0f, step), centre, weight)
            total += Tap(float2(0f, -step), centre, weight)
        }

        return total / max(weight, Const.Epsilon)
    }

    /// One bilateral tap, adding its weight to the running total.
    ///
    /// ⚠ `weight` is `inout`, which is what makes the caller's division correct: a tap rejected for
    /// being across an edge must not count in the divisor either, or a pixel beside a window would
    /// have its base divided by taps that contributed nothing and come out far too dark.
    func Tap(offset: float2, centre: float, inout weight: float): float {
        val at = uv + offset * texelSize

        if (!Fullscreen.InBounds(at)) {
            return 0f
        }

        val there = LogLuminance(source.Sample(sourceSampler, at).rgb)

        // The range term. `exp(-x²)` rather than a hard cutoff, because a threshold makes the base
        // discontinuous wherever the difference crosses it — which is a visible contour following the
        // shape of the edge, in a pass whose entire job is to not produce visible edges.
        val difference = (there - centre) / max(edgeRange, Const.Epsilon)
        val range = exp(-difference * difference)

        weight += range
        return there * range
    }

    /// Re-composes the image with its base compressed and its detail left alone.
    func Apply(): float4 {
        val colour = source.Sample(sourceSampler, uv)
        val luminance = ColorSpaces.Luminance(colour.rgb)

        if (luminance <= 0f) {
            return colour
        }

        val here = log2(max(luminance, Const.Epsilon))
        val slow = baseLuminance.Sample(baseSampler, uv).r

        // How far this region sits from where the meter says the middle is, and which way. The two
        // contrasts are separate because bringing a highlight down and lifting a shadow are not the
        // same operation with a sign — see the uniforms.
        val offset = slow - Pivot()
        val amount = offset > 0f ? saturate(highlightContrast) : saturate(shadowContrast)

        // ⚠ The *base* moves and the detail does not, which is the whole effect in one line. Scaling
        // `here` instead would compress the texture of the wall along with its brightness, and the
        // result is the flat grey look this exists to avoid.
        val moved = clamp(-offset * amount, -maximumStops, maximumStops)

        return float4(colour.rgb * exp2(moved), colour.a)
    }

    /// The log-2 scene luminance the compression pivots around — what is left exactly where it was.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **`0.18`, not the exposure equation's `1.2`.** They are both called middle grey and they are
    /// different numbers: 1.2 is ISO 2720's calibration constant, which appears in
    /// `exposure = 1 / (1.2 · 2^EV)` and maps an EV to the *output*; 0.18 is the reflectance that
    /// output lands on. The scene luminance that renders as middle grey is therefore `0.18 / exposure`
    /// — and anchoring the pivot to 1.2 instead puts it 2.74 stops too high, which drops nearly every
    /// texel in a photometric frame below it, where `shadowContrast` lifts the whole picture. That is
    /// a wash, and it is what this shader did.
    func Pivot(): float {
        val applied = max(UseExposureBuffer ? exposureBuffer[0] : exposure, Const.Epsilon)

        return log2(max(0.18f / applied, Const.Epsilon))
    }

    /// Log-2 luminance, floored so a black texel is a large negative number rather than minus infinity.
    func LogLuminance(colour: float3): float {
        return log2(max(ColorSpaces.Luminance(colour), Const.Epsilon))
    }
}
